::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles 48° and 67°. What is the third angle?
{
=A. 65°#Correct. 48 + 67 = 115, and 180 - 115 = 65.
~B. 55°#This subtracts incorrectly from 180°. Recheck the angle sum.
~C. 75°#This does not make the three angles total 180°.
~D. 115°#This is the sum of the two given angles, not the missing angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles 2x°, 3x°, and 4x°. What is x?
{
~A. 18#This would make the angle sum 162°, not 180°.
=B. 20#Correct. 2x + 3x + 4x = 180, so 9x = 180 and x = 20.
~C. 30#This would make the angle sum 270°.
~D. 45#This would make the angle sum 405°.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
An exterior angle of a triangle is 110°. One remote interior angle is 45°. What is the other remote interior angle?
{
~A. 55°#This does not add with 45° to make the exterior angle.
~B. 70°#This would make the remote interior sum 115°.
=C. 65°#Correct. By the Exterior Angle Theorem, 45 + x = 110, so x = 65.
~D. 155°#This adds the exterior angle and remote angle instead of subtracting.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
Which theorem explains why the interior angles of a triangle add to 180°?
{
~A. Exterior Angle Theorem#The Exterior Angle Theorem relates an exterior angle to two remote interior angles.
~B. Vertical Angles Theorem#Vertical Angles Theorem describes opposite angles formed by intersecting lines.
~C. Pythagorean Theorem#The Pythagorean Theorem relates side lengths in right triangles.
=D. Triangle Angle Sum Theorem#Correct. The Triangle Angle Sum Theorem states that triangle interior angles sum to 180°.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
An exterior angle is formed at one vertex. Which angles are remote interior angles?
{
=A. The two interior angles not adjacent to the exterior angle.#Correct. Remote interior angles are the two nonadjacent interior angles.
~B. The exterior angle and its adjacent interior angle.#The adjacent interior angle is not remote.
~C. Only the largest angle in the triangle.#Remote interior angles are based on position, not size.
~D. Any two angles that look equal.#Appearance does not define remote interior angles.
}

